Policy Memory (learned strategy rules):
1. Prioritize the highest similarity score only if it aligns with the visual evidence; otherwise, rely on detailed visual analysis. (Trigger: When the similarity scores are close and the visual evidence is ambiguous or conflicting.)
2. Focus on specific, unique morphological features when distinguishing between closely related species. (Trigger: When dealing with closely related species that share some common traits but have distinct morphological features.)
3. Focus on the unique combination of features such as the long, slightly curved beak and strong, dark-colored legs when identifying birds. (Trigger: This rule should be recalled whenever the bird's beak shape and leg strength are significant distinguishing factors.)

